{"sections":[],"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"major":0,"patch":0},"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/SwiftUsd\/documentation\/SwiftUsd\/GettingStartedInCPlusPlus"},"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Using SwiftUsd from C++"}],"seeAlsoSections":[{"title":"Using SwiftUsd from C++","anchor":"Using-SwiftUsd-from-C++","identifiers":["doc:\/\/SwiftUsd\/documentation\/SwiftUsd\/SwiftCxxInteropMemorySafety","doc:\/\/SwiftUsd\/documentation\/SwiftUsd\/SwiftCxxMacros"],"generated":true}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/SwiftUsd\/documentation\/OpenUSD"]]},"primaryContentSections":[{"content":[{"text":"Getting Started","type":"heading","anchor":"Getting-Started","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Before you can start using SwiftUsd, you need to add it as a dependency to your Xcode project or Swift Package, and then configure a few build settings.","type":"text"}]},{"style":"note","type":"aside","name":"Note","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Despite the fact that SwiftUsd is a Swift Package, you can use it in a pure-C++ Xcode project\/target, as well as in a pure-C++ Swift Package target."}]}]},{"style":"important","type":"aside","name":"Important","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Swift Package targets cannot mix Swift and C++ code in a single target. ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"https:\/\/github.com\/swiftlang\/swift-package-manager\/issues\/8945","isActive":true}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"To do so, follow the steps in "},{"isActive":true,"overridingTitle":"Getting Started for Swift","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftUsd\/documentation\/SwiftUsd\/GettingStarted#Getting-Started","overridingTitleInlineContent":[{"text":"Getting Started for Swift","type":"text"}],"type":"reference"},{"type":"text","text":". If you’re using a pure-C++ target, you don’t need to change the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"C++ and Objective-C Intero